WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:04.320
Sure, it's easy to understand why someone&nbsp;
would regret shooting a superhero flick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:09.240
rife with cheesy lines and marketing plugs. But&nbsp;
despising your part in one of the most beloved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:14.600
blockbusters of the '90s? You won't believe&nbsp;
some of the movies these actors regret filming.

00:00:14.600 --> 00:00:18.440
He may be one of Hollywood's biggest action&nbsp;
heroes, but Sylvester Stallone has had his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:23.480
failures too. From the much-maligned "Judge&nbsp;
Dredd" to the awkward '80s Dolly Parton drama&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:28.680
"Rhinestone," he probably regrets a great many&nbsp;
films in his career. But there's one '90s flop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:34.520
that stands head and shoulders above the rest: The&nbsp;
1992 action-comedy "Stop! Or My Mom Will Shoot."

00:00:34.520 --> 00:00:37.680
"Stop! Or My Mom Will Shoot" is best&nbsp;
known for being the film Stallone was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:41.440
tricked into making by none other than&nbsp;
action rival Arnold Schwarzenegger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:45.520
who pretended to be after the role&nbsp;
himself. He told The Hollywood Reporter,

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:48.280
"I had heard Schwarzenegger was&nbsp;
going to do that movie and I said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:51.120
'I'm going to beat him to&nbsp;
it.' I think he set me up."

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:54.480
Instantly regretting it, he was confused&nbsp;
by the rest of the film's casting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:58.160
particularly "Golden Girls"&nbsp;
star Estelle Getty. He recalled,

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:01.320
"[It] was supposed to be like 'Throw&nbsp;
Momma From the Train' with the mom as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:05.640
this really nasty piece of work. Instead,&nbsp;
you hire the nicest woman in Hollywood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:08.080
Estelle Getty, who you wish was your mother."

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:13.080
In the years since, Stallone has made his&nbsp;
hatred of the film known, telling GQ in 2017,

00:01:13.080 --> 00:01:15.080
"If you ever want someone to confess to murder,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:20.880
just make him or her sit through that film.&nbsp;
They will confess to anything after 15 minutes!"

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:24.840
The moment you hear the name "George Clooney" on&nbsp;
a list of regrettable movies, you probably already&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:29.560
know which movie is being lamented. It's one of&nbsp;
the most infamous comic book adaptations ever,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:35.640
a film so bad it killed a franchise and nearly&nbsp;
destroyed multiple careers. Released in 1997 and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:40.480
directed by Joel Schumacher, "Batman &amp; Robin" was&nbsp;
the fourth film in the series and Clooney's first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:45.520
big blockbuster project after starring in the&nbsp;
hit medical drama "ER." Needless to say, it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:54.520
also a notorious flop for a variety of reasons&nbsp;
– including a shameless American Express plug.

00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:55.920
"Never leave the cave without it."

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:58.560
Instead of making Clooney&nbsp;
a sought-after action hero,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:02.360
he had to spend years apologizing for&nbsp;
it. Clooney said in an appearance on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:07.280
the Graham Norton Show in 2015 that he always&nbsp;
apologizes for his involvement in the film.

00:02:07.280 --> 00:02:15.080
"But, you know, I thought at the time it was&nbsp;
going to be a very good career move. It wasn't."

00:02:15.080 --> 00:02:19.600
And he hasn't stopped trashing the film.&nbsp;
In 2020, in an interview with Howard Stern,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:23.880
he went further into just how awful&nbsp;
the flick really is. The star remarked,

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:28.200
"Akiva Goldsman — who's won the Oscar for&nbsp;
writing since then — he wrote the screenplay,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:31.560
and it's a terrible screenplay,&nbsp;
he'll tell you […] Joel Schumacher,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.560 --> 00:02:35.360
who just passed away, directed it,&nbsp;
and he'd say, 'Yeah, it didn't work.'"

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:37.960
But Clooney didn't just lay the&nbsp;
blame at the feet of others;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:41.880
he readily admits to his own failure&nbsp;
playing Bruce Wayne. He conceded,

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:48.320
"The truth of the matter is, I was bad&nbsp;
in it […] We all whiffed on that one."

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:53.600
Star of countless big screen classics, Tom Hanks&nbsp;
counts six Academy Award nominations to his name,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:58.040
including two wins. He also has seven&nbsp;
Primetime Emmy Awards as a producer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:03.000
on such favorites as "Band of Brothers" and&nbsp;
"Olive Kitteridge." Yet even with all his success,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:06.760
Hanks still has a movie that he finds&nbsp;
hard to stomach years later. Directed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:09.960
by Brian De Palma and co-starring&nbsp;
Bruce Willis, Melanie Griffith,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:14.680
and Morgan Freeman, "The Bonfire of the&nbsp;
Vanities" makes Hanks cringe to this day.

00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:19.920
A black comedy based on the celebrated 1987&nbsp;
novel by Tom Wolfe, the film sees Hanks play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:24.360
Sherman McCoy, a Wall Street power player,&nbsp;
husband, and father whose wealth isn't quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:29.400
what it seems. As his finances get stretched to&nbsp;
their limits, he accidentally kills a bystander&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:33.680
with his car while out with his mistress.&nbsp;
When this is discovered by a local attorney,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:38.440
it leads to a firestorm. It's a story with&nbsp;
a lot to say on racism, class inequality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.440 --> 00:03:42.160
and the excess of the '80s, but even&nbsp;
with all of its high-minded themes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.160 --> 00:03:46.840
it just isn't very good. He told Oprah&nbsp;
Winfrey in a 2001 interview in O,

00:03:46.840 --> 00:03:49.120
"It's one of the crappiest movies ever made."

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:52.880
And Hanks wasn't alone in this opinion:&nbsp;
The film was a box office bomb that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:57.360
was met with derision from critics,&nbsp;
making many "worst of the '90s" lists.

00:03:57.360 --> 00:04:01.840
Getting his start on TV in "The Fresh Prince of&nbsp;
Bel-Air," Will Smith shot to big screen stardom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:06.920
in the '90s with a string of blockbuster hits. He&nbsp;
had a four-year run of success that's hard to top,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:12.520
with "Bad Boys" in 1995, "Independence&nbsp;
Day" in 1996, "Men in Black" in 1997,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.520 --> 00:04:17.600
and the criminally underrated political thriller&nbsp;
"Enemy of the State" in 1998. Unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:21.880
his luck just couldn't hold, and in&nbsp;
1999, he made perhaps the worst film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.880 --> 00:04:27.880
of his career. He's said many times he wishes&nbsp;
he'd never made it. He told People in 2021,

00:04:27.880 --> 00:04:33.040
"'Wild Wild West' is a thorn in my side. To&nbsp;
see myself in chaps ... I don't like it."

00:04:33.040 --> 00:04:37.000
Why did he do the movie at all? Smith says&nbsp;
it was his own fault for getting carried&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:42.640
away by that incredible run of blockbusters.&nbsp;
Smith said in an interview with GQ in 2016,

00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:45.600
"I had so much success that I&nbsp;
started to taste global blood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.600 --> 00:04:49.400
and my focus shifted from my artistry to&nbsp;
winning [...] I found myself promoting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:53.800
something because I wanted to win, versus&nbsp;
promoting something because I believed in it."

00:04:53.800 --> 00:04:58.160
To make matters worse, "Wild Wild West" is the&nbsp;
movie Smith made after turning down the role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:03.560
of Neo in "The Matrix," a game-changingly&nbsp;
bad decision he still laments to this day.

00:05:03.560 --> 00:05:07.840
By 1995, Whoopi Goldberg had already long&nbsp;
established herself as one of the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:13.520
talented actresses in Hollywood. She received an&nbsp;
Academy Award nomination in 1986 for her leading&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:17.120
role in "The Color Purple" and took home&nbsp;
the trophy for best supporting actress in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:22.600
1991 for "Ghost." She'd featured in a recurring&nbsp;
role on the acclaimed sci-fi series "Star Trek:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:26.560
The Next Generation," appeared in a pair of&nbsp;
"Sister Act" movies, and was coming off her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:31.160
role as Shenzi in "The Lion King." That's&nbsp;
when she made easily the worst movie in her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.160 --> 00:05:35.200
career — and she knew she would regret it&nbsp;
the moment she stepped before the camera.

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:38.720
"He's a cop, we gotta make&nbsp;
him look like a real cop."

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:41.000
"I'm too sexy for my clothes."

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:45.120
That's because Goldberg's part in "Theodore&nbsp;
Rex" wasn't actually one that she wanted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:49.520
perform. She'd even gone so far as to try&nbsp;
to back out of the project, but was forced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:53.560
to follow through. She's never been shy about&nbsp;
expressing her disdain for the movie since,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.560 --> 00:05:58.240
either. During her days as a co-host on the&nbsp;
daytime talk show "The View," she once said,

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:00.400
"[The film] makes no sense to anyone."

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:05.080
Despite the fact that it has its fans, she&nbsp;
wishes it could be erased from existence.

00:06:05.080 --> 00:06:10.080
When it comes to superhero movies, there may be&nbsp;
no role more coveted than Batman — but it wasn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:15.560
always that way. Played by Adam West in the 1966&nbsp;
television series and an accompanying theatrical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.560 --> 00:06:20.280
release, it wasn't until Michael Keaton put&nbsp;
on the cape and cowl for Tim Burton's 1989&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:25.440
"Batman" that all of Hollywood took notice. So,&nbsp;
when Keaton stepped away after a pair of films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.440 --> 00:06:29.640
the role became a sought-after one. Val&nbsp;
Kilmer was as excited as you would imagine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.640 --> 00:06:34.440
to get behind the wheel of the Batmobile for&nbsp;
1995's "Batman Forever." But years before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:39.640
Clooney regretted its sequel, Kilmer ended up&nbsp;
having a miserable experience playing Batman.

00:06:39.640 --> 00:06:44.320
Unfortunately, along with Keaton, director Tim&nbsp;
Burton exited the threequel. Incoming helmer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:47.880
Joel Schumacher brought with him a very&nbsp;
different take on the character. That's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:52.320
when Kilmer's enthusiasm faded.&nbsp;
He said in his 2021 documentary

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:57.240
"Whatever boyhood excitement I had was crushed&nbsp;
by the reality of the Batsuit. Yes, every boy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.240 --> 00:07:02.760
wants to be Batman. They actually want to be&nbsp;
him ... not necessarily play him in a movie."

00:07:02.760 --> 00:07:05.920
But that's what Kilmer was doing, and&nbsp;
the critical drubbing the movie received&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.920 --> 00:07:09.920
further killed his interest in continuing&nbsp;
to play the character. Two years later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:14.240
Kilmer quit the series, unwilling to&nbsp;
debase himself further with another sequel.

00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:18.160
It's hard to imagine an actor regretting&nbsp;
not just the biggest role in their career,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:22.400
but a film that currently sits among the&nbsp;
highest-grossing ever made. Now imagine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:27.280
that film is also one of the most beloved films&nbsp;
in the history of cinema, with fans of all ages&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:32.840
passionate about its action, adventure, and&nbsp;
romance. What could there be to regret? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.840 --> 00:07:37.360
there's no need to imagine, because the movie&nbsp;
is James Cameron's "Titanic," and its star Kate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:41.600
Winslet who isn't so thrilled that people&nbsp;
are still rewatching it year after year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.600 --> 00:07:46.440
Not because the movie is bad, but because&nbsp;
she can't stand her own performance in it.

00:07:46.440 --> 00:07:50.240
"Stay back! Don't come any closer!"

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:54.280
Winslet said during press interviews&nbsp;
for the film's 3D re-release in 2012,

00:07:54.280 --> 00:07:57.920
"It's awful [...] It sounds terribly&nbsp;
self-indulgent but actors do tend to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.920 --> 00:08:01.760
be very self-critical. I have a hard&nbsp;
time watching any of my performances,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.760 --> 00:08:06.000
but watching 'Titanic' I was just like,&nbsp;
'Oh God, I want to do that again.'"

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:08.960
It's a performance — and film —&nbsp;
that she can barely sit through,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.960 --> 00:08:13.280
despite its lofty reputation and&nbsp;
millions of adoring fans. She mentioned,

00:08:13.280 --> 00:08:18.560
"Every single scene, I'm like 'Really,&nbsp;
really? You did it like that? Oh my God.'"

00:08:18.560 --> 00:08:23.280
Still, she has some consolation: Winslet was&nbsp;
nominated for an Academy Award for her role as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:28.520
Rose, though she might be glad she lost out to&nbsp;
Helen Hunt to avoid further attention for it.

00:08:28.520 --> 00:08:32.040
Sandra Bullock became one of Hollywood's&nbsp;
biggest rising starlets after appearing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.040 --> 00:08:36.800
opposite Sylvester Stallone in the 1993&nbsp;
action movie "Demolition Man." A year later&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:41.640
she starred with Keanu Reeves in the blockbuster&nbsp;
classic "Speed." But when a sequel was greenlit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:46.200
Reeves chose not to return. This gave Bullock&nbsp;
the rare chance to take a leading role in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.200 --> 00:08:50.320
her very own action movie, with Jason&nbsp;
Patric as her co-star. Unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.320 --> 00:08:55.040
when all was said and done, the movie was a&nbsp;
huge flop, barely making back its own budget.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.040 --> 00:08:59.360
And in the years since, the actress has admitted&nbsp;
just how much she wishes it had never happened.

00:08:59.360 --> 00:09:04.120
In 2022 she was asked by TooFab if there was&nbsp;
any movie in her career that she initially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.120 --> 00:09:09.480
didn't like, but that perhaps she'd learned to&nbsp;
appreciate because of its fan following. She said,

00:09:09.480 --> 00:09:14.760
"I have one no one came around to and I'm still&nbsp;
embarrassed I was in. It's called 'Speed 2.'"

00:09:14.760 --> 00:09:19.040
Admitting that the plot of a slow-moving boat&nbsp;
never really made a lick of sense to begin with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.040 --> 00:09:22.200
Bullock revealed that the entire&nbsp;
project was a shameful cinema sin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.200 --> 00:09:26.640
from top to bottom. After the interviewer&nbsp;
claimed they enjoyed the film, she said,

00:09:26.640 --> 00:09:35.160
"That's one I wished I hadn't done and no fans&nbsp;
came around, that I know of, except for you."

00:09:35.160 --> 00:09:39.160
Bruce Willis made a name for himself on the&nbsp;
small screen as Detective David Addison in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.160 --> 00:09:42.360
"Moonlighting" before making the&nbsp;
leap to the movies. When he did,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.360 --> 00:09:46.000
he became an instant star thanks to&nbsp;
1988's "Die Hard," where he played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:50.200
grizzled cop John McClane. He struggled&nbsp;
to recapture that film's success, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.200 --> 00:09:54.560
in disappointments like "Hudson Hawk" and&nbsp;
"Billy Bathgate." Perhaps trying to go back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.560 --> 00:09:59.120
to the kind of role that made him a movie star,&nbsp;
he became a cop in 1993's "Striking Distance."

00:09:59.880 --> 00:10:05.280
Willis plays a homicide detective named Tom Hardy,&nbsp;
but it isn't quite the same role as John McClane.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.280 --> 00:10:10.040
For one thing, Hardy is relegated to the role&nbsp;
of River Rescue Squad, far from the city-based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.040 --> 00:10:15.240
excitement of "Die Hard." The movie wasn't a hit,&nbsp;
and was mercilessly maligned by critics. When&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.240 --> 00:10:19.920
looking back on his career in a chat with Ain't&nbsp;
it Cool News, Willis threw the film under the bus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.920 --> 00:10:23.720
calling it a dog for which he hangs his&nbsp;
head in shame. Thankfully, it's a movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.720 --> 00:10:28.720
that most audiences have seemed to forget about,&nbsp;
being happier to focus on his winning entries.

00:10:28.720 --> 00:10:33.000
The 1994 romantic comedy "I Love&nbsp;
Trouble" had everything going for it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:36.920
starting with its star-studded cast.&nbsp;
Led by Julia Roberts and Nick Nolte,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.920 --> 00:10:40.480
it boasted a pair of big names who&nbsp;
producers dreamed would become a classic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.480 --> 00:10:45.120
Hollywood couple, ala Hepburn and Tracy.&nbsp;
Unfortunately, the result was a total dud,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.120 --> 00:10:49.280
with the two leads having fewer sparks than&nbsp;
a dead Zippo lighter. It completely bombed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.280 --> 00:10:53.960
at the box office. But that's not the only&nbsp;
reason why Julia Roberts regrets the film.

00:10:53.960 --> 00:10:57.080
Famously, Roberts and Nolte did not&nbsp;
get along at all on the set of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.080 --> 00:11:02.080
film. According to contemporary accounts in The&nbsp;
Los Angeles Times, the two feuded constantly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.080 --> 00:11:06.000
with Roberts more than a little annoyed&nbsp;
by his male chauvinism. The fact that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:06.000 --> 00:11:11.120
reportedly openly insulted her on set didn't&nbsp;
help. Rumors flew that things got so bad that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.120 --> 00:11:15.000
they refused to even be near each other,&nbsp;
often filming their scenes separately with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:19.080
stand-ins. Though these details were&nbsp;
never commented on by either of them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.080 --> 00:11:24.000
Roberts did openly call her co-star "completely&nbsp;
disgusting" in that Los Angeles Times interview,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.000 --> 00:11:28.760
and Nolte has since responded with equal contempt&nbsp;
for her. Suffice it to say, the production was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.760 --> 00:11:33.400
an extremely unpleasant one that Roberts&nbsp;
doesn't even like to talk about these days.

00:11:33.400 --> 00:11:38.920
In 1988, Bob Hoskins starred in one of the first&nbsp;
— and still best — movies to combine live-action&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.920 --> 00:11:43.920
and animation when he played Eddie Valiant in&nbsp;
"Who Framed Roger Rabbit?" But five years later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.920 --> 00:11:48.040
he tried to duplicate that success in another&nbsp;
family film ... and wound up leading a movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.040 --> 00:11:53.080
so famously awful it single-handedly&nbsp;
destroyed an entire genre. Later in life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.080 --> 00:11:58.160
Hoskins wasn't shy about discussing just how much&nbsp;
he regretted ever starring in the 1993 video game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.160 --> 00:12:04.360
adaptation of "Super Mario Bros." He said in a&nbsp;
wide-ranging interview with The Guardian in 2007,

00:12:04.360 --> 00:12:07.080
"The worst thing I ever did?&nbsp;
Super Mario Brothers. It was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.080 --> 00:12:13.480
a f—--g nightmare. The whole&nbsp;
experience was a nightmare."

00:12:13.480 --> 00:12:17.480
**screaming**

00:12:17.480 --> 00:12:21.080
He continued his expletive-laden rant&nbsp;
against the film, going on to say,

00:12:21.080 --> 00:12:25.880
"It had a husband-and-wife team directing, whose&nbsp;
arrogance had been mistaken for talent. After so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.880 --> 00:12:31.600
many weeks their own agent told them to get&nbsp;
off the set! F—--g* nightmare. F—--g idiots."

00:12:31.600 --> 00:12:35.320
Ultimately, the film wound up as poorly&nbsp;
received by audiences and critics as it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.320 --> 00:12:40.120
was by Hoskins. It gave the emerging genre&nbsp;
of video game adaptations a bad name,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.120 --> 00:12:43.720
and pretty much everyone involved&nbsp;
probably wishes it never happened.

00:12:43.720 --> 00:12:45.640
"I used to play King Lear."

00:12:45.640 --> 00:12:50.640
Since she rose to fame in the late '70s as Laurie&nbsp;
Strode in John Carpenter's "Halloween," Jamie Lee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.640 --> 00:12:54.880
Curtis has made scores of iconic films.&nbsp;
But she's made almost as many bad ones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.880 --> 00:12:59.880
too. In fact, Curtis has more than one film that&nbsp;
she's expressed regret over — and one of them was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.880 --> 00:13:05.800
a sequel to that John Carpenter original. We're&nbsp;
talking about "Halloween H20: 20 Years Later."

00:13:05.800 --> 00:13:09.800
After sitting out four "Halloween"&nbsp;
sequels, she agreed to return in 1998,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:09.800 --> 00:13:13.440
but quickly realized she'd&nbsp;
made a mistake. Curtis told EW,

00:13:13.440 --> 00:13:17.240
"To this day, I regret that I didn't say&nbsp;
to everyone, 'If Debra Hill's not the one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.240 --> 00:13:22.120
producing this movie, I'm not doing it. But what&nbsp;
ended up happening was, she wasn't part of it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.120 --> 00:13:27.920
John wasn't part of it, and I was still part of&nbsp;
it, and it was a machine going down the road."

00:13:27.920 --> 00:13:32.080
So far, we've covered a variety of reasons&nbsp;
actors regret even their biggest films.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.080 --> 00:13:37.000
But superstar Mark Wahlberg has a unique one,&nbsp;
and it involves his faith. A devout Catholic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.000 --> 00:13:41.000
there are movies from his early days in Hollywood&nbsp;
that he looks back on with serious misgivings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.000 --> 00:13:46.320
and chief among them is the 1997 comedy-drama&nbsp;
"Boogie Nights." It put the future A-lister&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.320 --> 00:13:50.600
into the role of an adult film star, and while&nbsp;
it's always been met with stellar reviews and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:50.600 --> 00:13:54.760
has a fervent fan following, it's not&nbsp;
something that Wahlberg is proud of.

00:13:54.760 --> 00:13:58.800
First came comments at a religious summit&nbsp;
in 2017 where the actor called out his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.800 --> 00:14:02.320
role as Diggler as something he&nbsp;
regrets. He told the audience,

00:14:02.320 --> 00:14:06.440
"I just always hope that God is a movie fan&nbsp;
and also forgiving because I've made some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.440 --> 00:14:10.720
poor choices in my past. 'Boogie Nights'&nbsp;
is up there at the top of the list."

00:14:10.720 --> 00:14:14.160
Quickly, those comments swept the web,&nbsp;
and Wahlberg had to explain further,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.160 --> 00:14:18.200
telling People Magazine that the role was one&nbsp;
that the Lord probably wasn't understanding of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.200 --> 00:14:20.680
given its extremely adult connotations.

00:14:20.680 --> 00:14:23.080
Wahlberg wasn't the only one to regret the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.080 --> 00:14:28.800
though. Co-star Burt Reynolds reportedly hated&nbsp;
the film so much that he fired his agent over it.

00:14:28.800 --> 00:14:31.720
We can all sympathize with an&nbsp;
actor who regrets a bad movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.720 --> 00:14:35.760
but when it comes to Ahmed Best, things&nbsp;
get downright heartbreaking. A theater&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.760 --> 00:14:39.760
performer whose career already included the&nbsp;
Broadway smash "Stomp," he was plucked from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:39.760 --> 00:14:44.120
the stage to play the first fully digital&nbsp;
main character in the "Star Wars" universe.

00:14:44.120 --> 00:14:45.840
But because he played Jar Jar Binks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.840 --> 00:14:51.400
Best became the target of fan outrage when the&nbsp;
first prequel film irked longtime diehards.

00:14:51.400 --> 00:14:53.680
"Uh, you'za might be sayin' that."

00:14:53.680 --> 00:14:58.200
The harassment Best received turned his&nbsp;
life upside down. Best said on Twitter,

00:14:58.200 --> 00:15:02.000
"I faced a media backlash that&nbsp;
still affects my career today"

00:15:02.000 --> 00:15:06.760
Most upsettingly, the actor admits that things&nbsp;
got so bad he seriously considered suicide.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.760 --> 00:15:10.760
Posting a photo of the New York harbor,&nbsp;
Best shared his story with his followers.

00:15:10.760 --> 00:15:14.760
"This was the place I almost ended my&nbsp;
life. It's still hard to talk about."

00:15:14.760 --> 00:15:17.040
Thankfully, Best was able to recover emotionally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.040 --> 00:15:21.120
but not without years of turmoil.&nbsp;
He shared with Wired in 2017,

00:15:21.120 --> 00:15:23.920
"There were a lot of tears,&nbsp;
there was a lot of pain."

00:15:23.920 --> 00:15:27.880
It's fair to wonder if he wouldn't have been&nbsp;
better off without the role entirely. Still,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.880 --> 00:15:32.680
he made his return to the franchise in&nbsp;
a 2023 episode of "The Mandalorian."

00:15:32.680 --> 00:15:36.440
"Star Trek" has a strong track record when it&nbsp;
comes to guest stars, going all the way back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.440 --> 00:15:40.240
to the original series, when Joan Collins&nbsp;
made an appearance in "City on the Edge of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:40.240 --> 00:15:44.720
Forever." The movies kept that tradition, with&nbsp;
Ricardo Montalban and Christopher Lloyd in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:44.720 --> 00:15:49.840
second and third films. Cinematic takes on "Star&nbsp;
Trek: The Next Generation" followed suit. It was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:49.840 --> 00:15:54.200
"Clockwork Orange" star Malcolm McDowell who&nbsp;
filled the role of celebrity guest in 1994's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.200 --> 00:15:58.600
"Star Trek Generations," but from the sound of it,&nbsp;
he wasn't a happy camper when he made the movie.

00:15:59.400 --> 00:16:02.920
According to McDowell, "Star Trek" was&nbsp;
stale and boring when he appeared in it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:02.920 --> 00:16:06.680
and the cast was even worse.&nbsp;
Reported by TrekMovie, he said,

00:16:06.680 --> 00:16:09.360
"I did them a favor [...] Patrick&nbsp;
Stewart spouting off for another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:09.360 --> 00:16:14.160
forty minutes. Jesus! If you find that&nbsp;
exciting, hey, go watch paint dry!"

00:16:14.160 --> 00:16:17.640
The film, which was met with tepid&nbsp;
reviews, was a modest success,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:17.640 --> 00:16:22.120
but clearly isn't one that McDowell looks&nbsp;
back on with any amount of fondness.

00:16:22.120 --> 00:16:24.920
Brad Pitt was just as huge&nbsp;
in the '90s as he is today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.920 --> 00:16:28.520
starring in classics like "Seven" "Interview&nbsp;
with the Vampire" and "Fight Club."

00:16:29.400 --> 00:16:32.800
"I want you to hit me as hard as you can."

00:16:32.800 --> 00:16:37.280
He's not without his clunkers though, and the&nbsp;
worst of them all might be an entry from 1997&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:37.280 --> 00:16:42.680
that the star himself seems to regret. Not just&nbsp;
because "The Devil's Own" was downright awful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.680 --> 00:16:45.240
but because its production was&nbsp;
plagued with problems from start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:45.240 --> 00:16:50.520
to finish — problems that famously included&nbsp;
frequent clashes with co-star Harrison Ford.

00:16:50.520 --> 00:16:53.720
All of those issues can probably be&nbsp;
traced to the fact that the project&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:53.720 --> 00:16:56.880
got greenlit without any plan. Pitt told Newsweek,

00:16:56.880 --> 00:17:00.640
"We had no script. Well, we had a great&nbsp;
script but it got tossed for various&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.640 --> 00:17:04.680
reasons. To have to make something up as you&nbsp;
go along […] It was the most irresponsible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:04.680 --> 00:17:07.960
bit of filmmaking — if you can even&nbsp;
call it that — that I've ever seen."

00:17:07.960 --> 00:17:09.280
He continued his rant,

00:17:09.280 --> 00:17:11.400
"I couldn't believe it. I don't know why anyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:11.400 --> 00:17:14.320
would want to continue making&nbsp;
that movie. We had nothing."

00:17:14.320 --> 00:17:18.280
At one point, Pitt became so frustrated&nbsp;
that he asked to get out of the movie.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:18.280 --> 00:17:21.600
But the studio wasn't budging and&nbsp;
demanded a massive multi-million&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:21.600 --> 00:17:25.360
dollar payout from their star if he&nbsp;
walked. In the end, the movie got made,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:25.360 --> 00:17:28.400
and while it paired two of Hollywood's&nbsp;
biggest names, it flopped in theaters.

00:17:29.560 --> 00:17:33.680
Co-founder of the British comedy troupe Monty&nbsp;
Python, Michael Palin starred in one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:33.680 --> 00:17:38.480
freshest comedies of the '80s, "A Fish Called&nbsp;
Wanda." An uproariously funny film co-starring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:38.480 --> 00:17:43.400
Kevin Kline, Jamie Lee Curtis, and his Python&nbsp;
cohort John Cleese — who also co-wrote and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:43.400 --> 00:17:49.200
co-directed it — it won Kline an Academy Award&nbsp;
for best actor and remains beloved decades later.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:49.200 --> 00:17:53.120
Though they never made a sequel, the entire&nbsp;
cast reunited for a spiritual successor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:53.120 --> 00:17:57.800
"Fierce Creatures," in 1997. But if&nbsp;
you ask Palin, it was a huge mistake.

00:17:57.800 --> 00:18:01.800
Interviewed by The Telegraph decades&nbsp;
later, Palin lamented the entire process&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:01.800 --> 00:18:05.400
of making the film. He described his&nbsp;
frustration at constant rewrites,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:05.400 --> 00:18:09.680
with Cleese even reducing his part&nbsp;
midway through filming. Palin remarked,

00:18:09.680 --> 00:18:11.920
"That it took four months of my life [to film] is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:11.920 --> 00:18:15.920
on the other hand, barely believable&nbsp;
from the amount of time on screen."

00:18:15.920 --> 00:18:20.040
Even when filming first wrapped, the&nbsp;
experience wasn't over. Not long before&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:20.040 --> 00:18:23.880
its planned release Palin was told that&nbsp;
early test screenings went poorly and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:23.880 --> 00:18:27.560
a wealth of reshoots were required.&nbsp;
With a newly recruited screenwriter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:27.560 --> 00:18:32.360
Cleese overhauled the script yet again. Palin&nbsp;
seemed proud of the final product ... until&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:32.360 --> 00:18:37.480
even more test screenings in New York forced&nbsp;
the whole thing to be cut down. Palin added,

00:18:37.480 --> 00:18:41.920
"After all these years of time, energy,&nbsp;
money and hard graft, 'FC' looks likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:41.920 --> 00:18:47.960
to be a 90-minute quickie, its shape and content&nbsp;
decided eventually by 20 people in Long Island."

00:18:47.960 --> 00:18:51.800
No, Ewan McGregor doesn't regret "Star&nbsp;
Wars: Episode I — The Phantom Menace"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:51.800 --> 00:18:55.760
the way his co-star Ahmed Best does.&nbsp;
After all, he was able to parlay the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:55.760 --> 00:19:00.800
part into his own Obi Wan-centric series&nbsp;
on Disney+ some 20 years later. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:00.800 --> 00:19:04.400
the Scottish actor who came to fame thanks to&nbsp;
indie hit "Trainspotting" looks back with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:04.400 --> 00:19:09.360
sense of disgust on the period drama "Emma,"&nbsp;
based on the classic novel by Jane Austen.

00:19:09.360 --> 00:19:12.080
Asked which work in his career&nbsp;
he'd consider his worst,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:12.080 --> 00:19:16.560
McGregor was quick to answer. He said&nbsp;
in 2003 in a piece for The Guardian,

00:19:16.560 --> 00:19:20.720
"Work wise? My character Frank Churchill&nbsp;
on 'Emma.' I made the decision to do that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:20.720 --> 00:19:24.520
film because I thought I should be seen to be&nbsp;
doing something different from 'Trainspotting."

00:19:24.520 --> 00:19:26.360
He continued to lament is role,

00:19:26.360 --> 00:19:29.640
"My decision-making was wrong [...] It's&nbsp;
a good film, 'Emma,' but I'm just ... not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:29.640 --> 00:19:33.800
very good in it. I'm not helped because&nbsp;
I'm also wearing the world's worst wig."

00:19:33.800 --> 00:19:38.000
That said, while he may regret his performance&nbsp;
and the film, McGregor said he used it as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:38.000 --> 00:19:41.480
valuable learning experience, and earned&nbsp;
a newfound understanding of how best to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:41.480 --> 00:19:47.000
pick his parts. Presumably, he now chooses&nbsp;
projects that offer him a better head of hair.

00:19:47.000 --> 00:19:49.800
Austrian&nbsp;
bodybuilder-turned-actor-turned-politician&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:49.800 --> 00:19:53.400
Arnold Schwarzenegger has a number of&nbsp;
films in his catalog he might wince at,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:53.400 --> 00:19:58.240
including his very first comedy, "Hercules in&nbsp;
New York." But that's not the one he regrets.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:58.240 --> 00:20:01.520
As a matter of fact, the one he seems&nbsp;
to have the most serious qualms about is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:01.520 --> 00:20:07.360
one that remains a cult favorite: The 1993&nbsp;
fantasy action-comedy, "Last Action Hero."

00:20:07.360 --> 00:20:13.320
"Help me! Get me out of here!&nbsp;
My God. This man's not dead!"

00:20:13.320 --> 00:20:17.600
A bomb in theaters, the film wasn't liked by&nbsp;
critics, and was seen by audiences as a huge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:17.600 --> 00:20:22.320
step down from his previous effort, "Terminator&nbsp;
2: Judgement Day." This reaction had the star&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:22.320 --> 00:20:26.600
rethinking his entire career. He said&nbsp;
in his Netflix documentary, 'Arnold,'

00:20:26.600 --> 00:20:30.800
"When 'Last Action Hero' came out, I had reached&nbsp;
my peak after 'Terminator 2', having the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:30.800 --> 00:20:34.960
successful movie of the year worldwide. So,&nbsp;
when the reviews for the film rolled out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:34.960 --> 00:20:40.080
I cannot tell you how upset that I was. It hurts&nbsp;
you. It hurts your feelings. It's embarrassing."

00:20:40.080 --> 00:20:43.800
Glum over the negative response,&nbsp;
Arnold withdrew completely. He said:

00:20:43.800 --> 00:20:45.920
"I didn't want to see anyone for a week."

00:20:45.920 --> 00:20:48.600
To cope, he relied on support&nbsp;
from his family and friends,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:48.600 --> 00:20:52.600
including frequent collaborator James&nbsp;
Cameron. Cameron said in the series,

00:20:52.600 --> 00:20:56.040
"He took it as a deep blow to his&nbsp;
brand. I think it really shook him."

00:20:56.040 --> 00:20:58.760
Thanks to that support, Arnold&nbsp;
was able to overcome his regrets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:58.760 --> 00:21:03.400
and team back up with Cameron for his&nbsp;
comeback a year later in "True Lies."

00:21:03.400 --> 00:21:07.040
British actor Hugh Grant has made plenty of&nbsp;
mistakes in his career. When it comes to his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:07.040 --> 00:21:11.480
movies, though, he definitely knows which one&nbsp;
he regrets most: "Nine Months." But it's not for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:11.480 --> 00:21:16.200
any of the usual reasons you'd expect. Despite it&nbsp;
being one of the worst movies in his filmography,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:16.200 --> 00:21:20.320
"Nine Months" isn't a movie he wishes he hadn't&nbsp;
done because it was bad, but because of who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:20.320 --> 00:21:26.040
financed it: controversial billionaire tycoon&nbsp;
Rupert Murdoch. He told Entertainment Weekly,

00:21:26.040 --> 00:21:29.880
"It would certainly stick in my craw to&nbsp;
work for Fox. "I did make ["Nine Months"]&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:29.880 --> 00:21:34.800
for them 16 years ago, but I was naïve then.&nbsp;
I didn't even know who owned [the studio]."

00:21:34.800 --> 00:21:38.280
The comments came in response to a phone&nbsp;
hacking scandal that rocked Britain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:38.280 --> 00:21:41.000
where the paper News of the World&nbsp;
was caught up in allegations that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:41.000 --> 00:21:44.640
it had tapped the phones of a number of&nbsp;
celebrities. Grant had helped catch them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:44.640 --> 00:21:48.760
in the act by recording a conversation with one&nbsp;
of their reporters, and went on record about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:48.760 --> 00:21:54.040
how disgusted he was by the paper's owner,&nbsp;
Murdoch, who also owned 20th Century Fox.

00:21:54.040 --> 00:21:58.400
If you or anyone you know is having suicidal&nbsp;
thoughts, please call the National Suicide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:58.400 --> 00:22:07.731
Prevention Lifeline by dialing 988&nbsp;
or by calling 1-800-273-TALK (8255)​.

